{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["And the expression <b>the headdresses of the turbans</b> — they went up on the head like ornaments (פְּאֵרִים), from the language of 'the ornaments and the bangles' (Isaiah 3:20), 'linen headdresses (פַּאֲרֵי פִשְׁתִּים) shall be on their heads' (Ezekiel 44:18). And he explained regarding the sash that they made it of <b>fine twisted linen and blue and purple and crimson</b>, and this was not specified in the commandment — only what is said of the sash: 'you shall make it embroidered work' (Exodus 28:39) — it is known that it is embroidered with these four kinds, as is said of the screen: 'And you shall make a screen for the entrance of the tent, of blue and purple and crimson, embroidered work' (Exodus 26:36). And this sash is also for Aaron and his sons, according to the opinion of the one who says (Yoma 12a): 'The sash of the High Priest — this is the sash of the ordinary priest.' And according to the opinion of the one who says (there): 'This is not the sash of the ordinary priest,' he specified regarding Aaron's that it is embroidered work, and there was no need to mention his sons' that it is linen, as He said: 'And for the sons of Aaron you shall make tunics, and you shall make them sashes, and you shall make them headdresses' (Exodus 28:40) — and all of them are linen like the tunics."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {0 "The Hebrew quotes 'יומא יב' and 'שם'; resolved to Yoma 12a (standard pagination for the Talmudic discussion of the priestly sash). The biblical citation 'שמות כ\"ו:ל\"ו' was already normalized in the text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:22:59.567653176Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}